course: religion-ethics
lesson: 50
title: Mock ethics olympiad
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In an ethics olympiad teams get a case, a short story with a hard choice, and discuss it in front of judges; they score for good thinking, not for "winning" an argument.
explain: First the team reads the case together, underlines the key facts and lists the stakeholders, everyone the choice affects; then it puts the ethical question into one clear sentence.
explain: The team builds a view with reasons, says honestly where members disagree, and each member presents a short part in turn.
explain: Judges ask questions: listen, think, then answer directly; saying "we had not considered that" is better than bluffing, and when the other team speaks you begin with what was good in their view.
explain: A Christian child may use the Good Samaritan or other teaching, but should explain the reason so that people of any faith or none can follow it, and afterwards the team reviews its round and thanks everyone, because character counts as much as the score.
fact: In an ethics olympiad teams discuss a case in front of judges and are scored on the quality of their thinking.
fact: Reading the case means finding the key facts and listing the stakeholders, everyone the choice affects.
fact: Preparation is half the work.
fact: The ethical question should be put in one clear sentence, because clear questions lead to clear answers.
fact: A team agrees a view with reasons, and honest disagreement within the team can be shared.
fact: Each team member presents part of the argument clearly and briefly.
fact: When judges ask a question, the team listens, thinks and answers directly.
fact: Saying "we had not considered that" impresses more than bluffing.
fact: When responding to another team, you begin with what was good in their view, then add to it or question it respectfully.
fact: Olympiads reward collaboration, not putting the other team down.
fact: Teams may draw on religious teaching, philosophy, law or experience, explaining why it matters to the case.
fact: Reasons should be understandable to people of any faith or none.
fact: After a round the team reviews what went well and what to improve.
fact: Winning well means thanking the judges and the other teams and celebrating good thinking on all sides.
exercise: What does the team do first when it gets the case? | Reads it together, underlines key facts and lists the stakeholders | Decides the answer before even reading the case, just to save time | Picks the loudest member to speak for everyone | Looks up what the other team thinks
exercise: Who are the stakeholders in a case? | Everyone the choice affects | Only the people who are the richest | Only the people who will judge the case | Only the person who has to choose
exercise: A case: Mia sees her friend copy in a test. What is the ethical question in one clear sentence? | Should Mia tell the teacher, talk to her friend, or say nothing? | Why are tests so hard these days? | Is Mia's friend good at maths? | What did Mia have for lunch that day?
exercise: Why should the ethical question be put in one clear sentence? | Clear questions lead to clear answers | Judges only have time to read one line | Long questions are against the rules | So that nobody can disagree with it
exercise: Two members of your team disagree. What should you do in the round? | Share the team's view and say honestly where you disagree | Hide the disagreement and pretend you all agree | Let the two members argue it out between themselves in front of the judges | Leave the disagreeing member out of the team
exercise: How can you keep your part under one minute? | Plan your main point and two reasons, and practise saying them | Speak as fast as you possibly can | Read the whole case out word for word | Leave out all of the reasons and just give the answer as fast as you can
exercise: Why does each member present a part in turn? | It shares the floor and keeps each part clear and brief | So the judges cannot tell who is the best speaker in the team | Because only one person is allowed to think | So the round lasts as long as possible
exercise: How should you answer a question you have not thought about? | Pause, think honestly, and say "we had not considered that" if needed | Make up something that sounds clever | Change the subject to something you know | Say the question is stupid and move on
exercise: Why does honesty impress judges more than bluffing? | Judges score good thinking, and honest thinking shows it | Judges cannot really tell whether you are bluffing or not | Bluffing is fine as long as you speak loudly and confidently | Honesty just makes the round end more quickly for everyone
exercise: What would you say first when responding to another team? | Point out what was good in their view | Point out every single mistake they made | Say your team is obviously better than theirs | Say nothing at all and wait for the judges
exercise: How could you use the Good Samaritan without assuming everyone is Christian? | Tell the story and explain its point, that we should help anyone in need | Say that everyone must believe the Bible or lose | Use it without saying what it means | Say that only Christians can help strangers
exercise: Which sources may a team draw on? | Religious teaching, philosophy, law or experience, explained clearly | Only the Bible, with no explanation | Only what the judges have said before | Only rumours from social media
exercise: Why should reasons be understandable to people of any faith or none? | So that everyone in the room can weigh and judge them | Because faith must never be mentioned at an olympiad | Because judges at an olympiad are never religious people | Because reasons should be as short as possible
exercise: What would you do after the round? | Review together what went well and what to improve | Blame the member who made a mistake in front of everyone | Forget it straight away and never think about it again | Complain to the judges about the score you were given
exercise: Why do olympiads give awards for good sportsmanship? | Character counts as much as the score | To make the losing teams feel less upset | Because nobody can agree who won | To reward the team that talked the most
exercise: What does winning well look like? | Thanking judges and teams and celebrating good thinking on all sides | Cheering loudly every time the other team stumbles or forgets a word | Telling the other team they never had a chance | Leaving quickly before anyone else is thanked
test: Teammate: "Don't bother reading the case properly, we'll just wing it." What is the best reply? | Preparation is half the work; let's find the key facts first | Good idea, the judges always like quick answers better than slow ones | Fine, but only if we talk very fast | Yes, reading the case is the judges' job
test: A judge asks, "What about the friend's feelings?" and your team never thought of it. What is best? | Say honestly you had not considered it, then think it through aloud | Pretend you already discussed it and invent a clever answer on the spot | Say the friend's feelings don't matter | Ask the judge to ask a different question
test: The other team's speaker says something good but misses a point. Which response wins most credit? | "We liked your point about fairness; we'd add the friend's safety" | "That was totally wrong and a bit silly" | "Our team obviously thought of all this first, long before you did" | "We have nothing to say about your view"
test: A Jewish judge asks, "Why should the Good Samaritan matter to someone who isn't Christian?" Which answer is best? | Its lesson, helping anyone in need whoever they are, makes sense to people of any faith | It doesn't matter to you, it's only for Christians | Because the Bible says so, and that's that | Because Christians are the best at helping
test: "We lost, the judges were total idiots and the other lot cheated." What is wrong with this response? | It attacks people instead of learning from the round | It is far too kind and grateful to everyone who was there | It gives far too many good reasons for losing the round | Nothing; it is the natural and fair thing to say after losing
test: Your teammate says, "The stakeholders are just the person making the choice." How do you put it right? | Stakeholders are everyone the choice affects | Stakeholders are the people holding the sticks | Stakeholders are only the judges and the teams | Stakeholders are the people who own a business
test: Teammate writes the question as: "Is it about lying and stuff and friends and also maybe the teacher?" How could it be improved? | Put it in one clear sentence, like "Should Sam tell the truth if it hurts a friend?" | Make it longer so nothing is left out | Turn it into a list of ten questions | Leave it as it is, the judges will understand
test: "ugh I always talk for like 5 mins, it's fine right?" What is the best advice? | Keep your part short so teammates get their turn and the point stays clear | Yes, the longer you speak, the more points you get from the judges, always | Yes, if you speak fast it does not count | Yes, the others can just speak less
test: A judge's follow-up: "Your team disagreed. Isn't that a weakness?" Which answer is best? | Not necessarily: we weighed both sides and are open about where we differ | Yes, so we will throw out our quietest member | We didn't disagree at all, so the judge must simply have misheard what we said | It's a weakness for the other team, not for us
test: After the round, which question is most useful for the team to ask? | What would we do differently next time? | Whose fault was it that we didn't win? | How can we beat that team next time by any means? | Which judge didn't like us?
test: Which team behaviour do olympiad judges reward? | Listening well, building on others' ideas and giving reasons | Interrupting often, so that the other team cannot finish their point | Repeating the same point a little louder each time you speak | Agreeing with whatever the judge seems to think about the case
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the criterion upon which teams in an ethics olympiad are assessed? | The quality of the reasoning exhibited, together with the conduct of the discussion | The volume at which the members of the team express their views | The number of times the opposing team is shown to be mistaken | The degree to which the team's view accords with that of the judges
exam: Identify the response most appropriate where a judge advances a consideration which the team has not previously examined. | An honest acknowledgement of the omission, followed by reasoned reflection upon it | The fabrication of an answer intended to conceal the omission | A refusal to address the consideration so advanced | The redirection of the discussion towards some more familiar and comfortable subject
exam: Whereas a team draws upon the parable of the Good Samaritan, select the manner of its use most consistent with good practice. | Its significance is to be explained in terms intelligible to persons of any faith or none | It is to be cited as binding upon all, irrespective of belief | It is to be cited without any account of its meaning | It is to be employed so as to disparage persons of other beliefs
exam: State the principal reason for which olympiads confer awards in recognition of good sportsmanship. | Character is held to be of no less account than the score attained | Such awards serve solely to console the teams which are defeated | The assessors are otherwise unable to determine a victor | Such awards are conferred upon the team which speaks at greatest length
